# __PRODUCT_NAME__

A Harmos guest plugin exposing the local operation `greeting.greet`.
`operations/greeting/` owns the request, declaration and runtime implementation.
`domain/model/` owns shared greeting values and pure behavior. Disable default
features to use contracts alone; `runtime` enables execution and the carrier.

```sh
mise install
mise run prepare
cargo generate-lockfile
mise run check
mise run build
mise run build:rust:tests
mise run test:rust:unit
```

`lint:rust` runs Clippy with warnings denied, included by `check:rust` and
gated by CI Check / Rust. Build, test, bench and fixture tasks do not run Clippy.
A focused `clippy` operation is an explicit choice. Use `mise run lint:rust --extended`
for optional compatibility lint coverage.

The scaffold uses the explicitly selected Harmos source revision or local checkout.
Install `cargo-harmos` to run `mise run dev`; it is the development tool used to create this project.
Keep generated tooling current with `stkr project update` when adopting company tooling changes.

## Operation Context

Each operation file owns its request, response, policy and execution. Shared data
lives in `domain`; plugin lifecycle preparation owns resources. Calls descend
from higher-level operations to lower-level operations without cycles. Driver
results cross the operation boundary as plugin-owned messages.

Orchestration reads top to bottom under Title Case action comments: construct
`let request = ...;`, then invoke `ctx.operation(request).await?;`. Repeat actions
in the flow; keep reusable library logic with its owner. Compute named checks
before combining them, and suffix physical quantities with their units. Callees
own timeouts, delays and retries; callers override policy only for one call.


Operations consume their request in `run(self, ctx: harmos::Context<'_, Self>)`. Declare `resources` and `operations` on the operation; use
`ctx.resource::<T>()`, `ctx.operation(request).await?` and `ctx.streams()`. The shared SDK `Invocation` also accepts `.retry(policy)`,
`.endpoints(...)` and `.start()`. Policy defaults belong to the operation macro; take retry overrides from settings or configuration.
Await returns `Response<T>`: borrow it like the value, or use `into_owned()` to take the payload. Declared `operations` still authorize child calls.
Plugin lifecycle hooks use `harmos::guest::kit::Context`; import `harmos::guest::OperationHost` to invoke operations on that carrier host.

## Plugin Lifecycle

`Lifecycle::prepare(config, setup)` constructs the plugin and registers shared resources
with `setup.resource(value)`. `Plugin::CATALOGS` declares its operation catalogs.
The owner validates setup before `initialize`, then drains work before `stop` and
`cleanup` during shutdown. Use the `harmos` facade for contracts, execution and carrier APIs.
